# NOBORU BACKEND RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: CRITICAL

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs all server-side business logic, API routes, and service-layer implementation within Noboru.

Every service, API handler, workflow orchestrator, and server action that performs domain work must follow this document.

architecture.mdc is the parent authority for layering and domain ownership.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* Business logic lives in services, not UI
* All APIs are typed, validated, and permission-aware
* Data access remains in repositories
* Features remain isolated at the service boundary
* Server behavior is testable and reusable
* Future Supabase Edge Functions can be introduced without rewriting domains

Build services.

Not endpoints with embedded logic.

⸻

REQUIREMENTS

Layered Flow

Mandatory order:

Component or Route Handler

↓

Service

↓

Repository

↓

Database

Services

* Own business rules, validation, and orchestration
* Must be reusable across pages, APIs, background jobs, and future edge functions
* Must not render UI
* Must not perform raw SQL or Supabase queries directly when a repository exists

Repositories

* Own all persistence and query logic
* Must not contain product rules or workflow orchestration

API Routes

* Located under app/api/ grouped by domain
* Must validate input
* Must validate permissions and ownership
* Must return typed responses
* Must handle errors consistently
* Must log failures without leaking secrets

Types

* All public service methods require typed inputs and outputs
* Forbidden: any without explicit justification
* Shared domain types live in feature types/ or lib/ as appropriate

Validation

* Validate at system boundaries: API routes and service public methods
* Use schemas where the codebase establishes them
* Reject invalid input early with actionable errors

Authorization

* Every user-owned resource requires ownership validation
* Never trust client-provided user IDs without session verification
* RLS is required at the database layer; services must not rely on RLS alone

Future Edge Functions

* Domain logic must remain portable to Supabase Edge Functions
* Keep side effects and external integrations isolated
* Do not embed Next.js-specific assumptions into core service logic

Error Handling

* Distinguish validation, authorization, not-found, conflict, and system errors
* Return stable error shapes to callers
* Never expose internal stack traces or secrets to clients

⸻

CODING STANDARDS

Service Design

* One service per domain concern
* Methods should be small, composable, and named by intent
* Prefer pure functions for calculations; isolate I/O in repositories

API Design

* Group routes by feature domain, not HTTP verb folders
* Use consistent response envelopes where established
* Map service errors to correct HTTP status codes

Dependencies

* Services may depend on repositories and other services through clear interfaces
* Forbidden: service-to-service hidden coupling across unrelated features
* Forbidden: circular dependencies

Logging

* Log failures with domain context
* Never log tokens, passwords, or full PII

Testing Hooks

* Services must be unit-testable without rendering UI
* Repositories must be mockable at service boundaries

Supabase Usage

* Server-side Supabase clients belong in repository or infrastructure layers
* API routes call services; services call repositories

⸻

SUCCESS CRITERIA

Backend work is successful when:

* Business logic exists in one authoritative service location
* API routes are thin orchestrators
* All inputs and outputs are typed
* Authorization is enforced before mutations
* Repositories contain all database access
* Errors are handled consistently
* The same service can be reused from UI workflows and API endpoints
* Future edge deployment requires movement, not rewrite

⸻

FAILURE CRITERIA

Backend quality is degraded when any of the following occur:

* Database queries inside API route handlers beyond repository delegation
* Business rules duplicated in pages, hooks, or components
* Untyped API responses
* Missing permission checks
* Services that know about React or UI state
* Repositories containing scoring, gamification, or lesson logic
* Leaked secrets or environment values in responses or logs
* Feature services importing each other's internals directly

When failure criteria appear, extract services and repositories before extending behavior.

⸻

EXAMPLES

Correct API route

app/api/review/submit/route.ts validates session, parses body, calls reviewService.submitAnswer(), returns typed result.

Correct service

features/review/services/review.service.ts calculates next review state and delegates persistence to review.repository.ts.

Correct repository

features/review/repositories/review.repository.ts performs Supabase queries only.

Incorrect route

Route handler inserts directly into Supabase and computes SRS intervals inline.

Incorrect service

Service imports a React hook or reads window.

Incorrect cross-layer call

Component imports repository and bypasses service.

⸻

ANTI-PATTERNS

Fat API routes with embedded business logic

Direct supabase.from() calls in route handlers when a repository exists

Duplicated validation in UI and API with divergent rules

Services returning any

Authorization checks only in the frontend

Business logic in repositories

Feature coupling through deep imports

Logging access tokens or refresh tokens

Putting Next.js request parsing logic inside reusable domain services without abstraction

END OF backend.mdc
